{"type": "spr", "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper L\"><mi>L</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"60\"><mn>60</mn>\n</math> degrees. If the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper L\"><mi>L</mi>\n</math> is <math alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over n EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></math> <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">radians</span>, where <math alttext=\"n\"><mi>n</mi>\n</math> is a constant, what is the value of <math alttext=\"n\"><mi>n</mi>\n</math>?</p>", "keys": ["3"], "rationale": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math>. The measure of an angle in radians is equal to the measure of the angle in degrees multiplied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 180 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>180</mn></mfrac></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper L\"><mi>L</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60\"><mn>60</mn></math> degrees. Multiplying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60\"><mn>60</mn></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 180 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>180</mn></mfrac></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 60 pi Over 180 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>60</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>180</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. It&rsquo;s also given that the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper L\"><mi>L</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over n EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></math> radians, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction pi Over n EndFraction equals StartFraction pi Over 3 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"pi\"><mi>&#960;</mi></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 1 Over n EndFraction equals one third\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. Taking the reciprocal of both sides of this equation yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction n Over 1 EndFraction equals three oneths\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"n equals 3\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>. Therefore, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"n\"><mi>n</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math>.</p>", "externalid": "7ceef3d0-2aeb-40e2-a3fe-45da4ef0f952", "correct_answer": ["3"]}